      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#0 · the authored whole; Nietzsche and Gebser in distinct registers",
          "title": "A world becomes a view",
          "italic": "Apollo embodies form's gift: the image lets one person show another what has been seen, and gives the seer something definite to revisit and change.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "A finite being gives the world a form it can inhabit and communicate. Something stands out; a boundary makes it distinct; an image holds it long enough to become intelligible. In Taylor's Mytheme, Apollo names this achievement. The image lets one person show another what has been seen without requiring them to have occupied the same place — and gives the seer something definite to revisit, question and change. This is the explicitly composed whole of Taylor's Apollo–Dionysus–Daphne programme; the combination with Daphne belongs to this authored Mytheme, and its sequence takes place in the activity of seeing, participating and answering.",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.55,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 620
        },
        {
          "id": "occ",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "Occurrences: the standing-out and its boundary (#0); the communicable image (#0, #1); the revisitable view (#0, #5→0).",
          "x": 0.765,
          "y": 0.55,
          "width": 300,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
