      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#2 · Nietzsche Birth of Tragedy §1 (p.21) and §21 (p.167); the central plan's dismemberment",
          "title": "Dionysus opens the enclosure and returns through form",
          "italic": "Dionysus names the release through which the view's implication in life becomes available again — within this composition, what dies is the form's claim, and what returns must be communicable enough to answer for.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "Participation restores the view to the living relation its self-sufficiency concealed. The central plan gives this release the force of dismemberment and death/rebirth: within this Taylor-authored sequence, what dies is the form's claim to stand complete in itself; what returns must acquire a form through which living relation can continue. Nietzsche supplies the artistic relation: in §1, art develops through the duplexity of the Apollonian",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "and Dionysian; in §21, Dionysus speaks Apollo's language and Apollo finally speaks Dionysus's. The formed account must let its participation affect what it says, and participation must become communicable enough for a particular speaker to answer for it. Yet a moment of openness does not settle the question: participation can itself become an overriding image, an assertion of unity deciding in advance what another being's difference must mean.\n\nOccurrences: the opened enclosure (#2); Dionysian dismemberment and reconstitution (#2); reciprocal expression, §21 (#2); fusion as the pathology distinguished in the programme (#2, #3).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
