      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#3 · the alterity check (human-amplified: yes); the Ovidian whole kept distinct",
          "title": "Daphne requires someone to remain other",
          "italic": "Daphne proves-discloses the difference between failures: appropriation through determination and appropriation through undifferentiation — her separate literary whole bears its own cast, pursuit, transformation and afterlife.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "Daphne changes what the mutuality must accomplish: a relation can be vivid and shared while still failing to let the other answer in their own right. Apollo cannot make illumination into entitlement; Dionysus cannot make participation into permission to absorb. Her office is the particular centre whose existence exceeds what either power makes of it. Her separate literary whole is Ovid's Apollo, Eros, Daphne and Peneus — its own cast, pursuit,",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "transformation and public laurel afterlife; the movement-35 return preserves the tree's recoil together with the narrator's seeming assent, so no easy tale of union or final refusal replaces the source. What this composition receives is appropriation after withdrawal: the failure of possession at one level need not end the image's symbolic continuation. She asks of participation equally: unity invoked to cancel refusal has repeated the appropriation by other means.\n\nOccurrences: Daphne's required otherness (#3); the recoil and seeming assent kept together (#3, via movement 35); Dionysus' entry through Taylor's composition, not Ovid's cast (#0, #3).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
