      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#4 · Gebser pp.1–3, 24–28, 470–86 (paraphrased); Q27 180°→360°; P5 third/fourth-person",
          "title": "The view becomes transparent to its context",
          "italic": "The diaphanous form embodies determination visible together with the conditions through which it became a view — promising no exhaustive panorama.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The resulting form is diaphanous: its determination remains visible together with the conditions through which it has become a view. Gebser's aperspectivity releases perspective from exclusive validity without amalgamating earlier structures; his Picasso/Braque discussion makes time and multiple aspects material to representation. Q27's contextual-field programme gives the movement its 180°→360° expression: the perspectival triangle opens",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "into a field where positions, counter-positions and transitions are available together — and the knower has a position within that field. A wider collection of views cannot accomplish this by accumulation; their limits, relations and means of transformation have to become intelligible. P5's concern gives transparency a lived direction: equanimity requires acknowledging concern; the pose of having no stake can conceal a governing preference.\n\nOccurrences: the triangle's opening (#4); the viewer's implication disclosed alongside the viewed (#1, #4); the absent-ground movement returned as owned finite judgment (#1, #4).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
